                <pb facs="1010" n="878" sameAs="frus:frus1926v01/pg878"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.512/412: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Mayer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-10-14T14:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 14,
                                1926—2 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 14—5:50
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>478. My 469, October 12, 11 a.m.<anchor n="64" xml:id="d662-f1"/> Following from
                    American consul general, Canton: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“October 13, 3 p.m. Referring to my telegram of October 11, noon.<anchor
                                n="64" xml:id="d662-f2"/> New taxes are now being collected on
                            imports and exports. All China [<hi rendition="#italic">Chinese</hi>]
                            and some foreigners are paying, but other foreigners are asking their
                            consuls for instructions.</p>
                        <p>“Canton foreign Chambers [of] Commerce have written consular corps
                            recommending that new taxes be collected through the Maritime Customs or
                            on values fixed by Customs.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mayer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
